Root cause:
The bug arises from a use-after-free condition in the `tcpm_register_source_caps` function. When new source capabilities are registered, the existing capabilities are unregistered first. If the registration of the new capabilities fails (for example, due to invalid input), the pointer `port->partner_source_caps` still references the memory of the now-freed source capabilities, leading to potential access of invalid memory.

Code statements information:
The code statements that lead to the bug are:
```c
usb_power_delivery_unregister_capabilities(cap);
```
```c
cap = usb_power_delivery_register_capabilities(port->partner_pd, &caps);
```
```c
port->partner_source_caps = cap;
```
Reason:
The bug occurs because when `usb_power_delivery_unregister_capabilities(cap)` is called, it frees the memory associated with `cap`. If the subsequent registration of new capabilities fails, `port->partner_source_caps` still points to the now-freed memory, leading to a use-after-free condition when it is accessed later.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```c
port->partner_source_caps = NULL;
```
Reason:
This statement resets `port->partner_source_caps` to `NULL` after unregistering the existing source capabilities. This ensures that if the registration of new capabilities fails, `port->partner_source_caps` does not reference freed memory, thus preventing any potential use-after-free access.


